// What moves, once the events are known. The last event that must be named is
// the point every earlier effect has to stay ahead of: a call, an index, an
// operator that can trap, or an import a moving call could change, evaluated
// before it, moves too, in the same order. What is left in the statement was
// evaluated after all of them, or has no effect and reads nothing a moved
// call can write -- a local, a parameter, a literal, a non-trapping operator
// over those -- and so gives the same value either way. Decided in fix_plan's
// chain (../../walk/roots/simple.id) once its own block is full.
fix_decide2(int sn, int md, int last) {
  if(md > 0) {
    fix_refuse_md(md, last);
  } else {
    fix_mark(sn, last);
  }
} return void;

fix_mark(int sn, int last) {
  fix_mark_moves(last);
  fix_mark_sep(last);
  string why = fix_why_cond(last);
} chain (int sn, int last) {
  if(why == "") {
    why = fix_why_type(sn, last);
  }
  if(why == "") {
    fix_emit_stmt(sn, last);
  } else {
    fix_refuse(last, why);
  }
} return void;

// fix_mark_cls (one.id) records its answer in place: it lsets a 1 into a cell
// that already exists, so the column's length never changes and fixe_mv_len
// (moves.id) cannot see it happen. Summing the column can, because a cell
// that changes changes the total. It reports that some event was marked, not
// which one, so it anchors a regression rather than pinning a cell -- a sum
// that stayed still would also hide two writes that cancelled, which this
// column cannot do, since the only value ever written to it here is 1.
fixe_mv_sum() {
  int n = 0;
  int i = 0;
  while(i < len((import fixe_mv))) {
    n = n + (import fixe_mv)[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
} return int n;
